          "text": "Level: B1 · Topic: The Market",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "Level: B1 · Topic: The Market"
        },
        {
          "text": "Shopping at the Market\n\nEvery Saturday morning, I go to the outdoor market in the town centre. It is much bigger than a supermarket, and I enjoy the atmosphere — the colours, the smells, and the sound of vendors calling out their prices.\n\nThe fruit and vegetable stalls are always the busiest. Fresh apples, oranges, bananas, and berries are arranged in neat rows. The vegetables — tomatoes, potatoes, onions, peppers, and lettuce — are weighed on a scale, and you pay by the kilogram.\n\nNext to the produce section, there is a butcher's stall with different cuts of meat: chicken, lamb, and beef. Across from it, the fishmonger sells fresh fish, shrimp, and squid laid out on ice. The cheese stall offers dozens of varieties, and you can taste samples before buying.\n\nThere is also a bakery stall with fresh bread, pastries, and cakes. The smell of warm bread fills the air every morning. Nearby, a spice vendor sells bags of cumin, turmeric, cinnamon, and dried herbs.\n\nI always bring my shopping bag and a list. I compare prices between stalls before choosing. Some vendors give you a discount if you buy in bulk. At the end of the morning, when the market is about to close, you can sometimes get a bargain.\n\nI pay with cash at most stalls, but some now accept card payments. The market is not only a place to shop — it is a social experience where neighbours meet, chat, and share recipes.",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "Shopping at the Market Every Saturday morning, I go to the outdoor market in the town centre. It is much bigger than a supermarket, and I enjoy the atmosphere — the colours, the smells, and the sound of vendors calling out their prices. The fruit and vegetable stalls are always the busiest. Fresh apples, oranges, bananas, and berries are arranged in neat rows. The vegetables — tomatoes, potatoes, onions, peppers, and lettuce — are weighed on a scale, and you pay by the kilogram. Next to the produce section, there is a butcher's stall with different cuts of meat: chicken, lamb, and beef. Across from it, the fishmonger sells fresh fish, shrimp, and squid laid out on ice. The cheese stall offers dozens of varieties, and you can taste samples before buying. There is also a bakery stall with fresh bread, pastries, and cakes. The smell of warm bread fills the air every morning. Nearby, a spice vendor sells bags of cumin, turmeric, cinnamon, and dried herbs. I always bring my shopping bag and a list. I compare prices between stalls before choosing. Some vendors give you a discount if you buy in bulk. At the end of the morning, when the market is about to close, you can sometimes get a bargain. I pay with cash at most stalls, but some now accept card payments. The market is not only a place to shop — it is a social experience where neighbours meet, chat, and share recipes."
        },
        {
